>>:  Of course knight must be lawful.
>>Why cant you play an evil knight?
My gut reaction is to point out that Chaotic doesn't mean Evil, that
in D&D (which large portions of NetHack is based on) the Law/Chaos axis
is completely seperate from the Good/Evil axis, that various works of
fantasy (notably some of Moorcock's Elric stuff, which predates D&D)
show Law and Chaos as both having good and bad aspects, that the Erisians
consider Chaos to be good (essential to anything creative, and far too
underemphasized in any case).
Then I remembered that, in NetHack, Chaos practically _is_ synonymous with
Evil. Oh well.
I still don't see why we can't have chaotic knights, though.  What about
Sir Breuse Sans Pitie? He always seemed pretty chaotic to me.  (In
certain Arthurian legends, Sir Breuse was a rogue knight who spent his
time taking knights by surprise, knocking them off their horses, and then
running away before they could retaliate.)

